WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.100
Let your with us on First Principles Daily Episode 21.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.500
Friday, let's wrap the week reasoning from the ground up.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.820
Before 1909, nitrogen for crops came from finite mines

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:12.020
and seabird droppings.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.680
The Haber Bosch process turned the air itself

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.420
into an unlimited supply.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.980
Before the 20th century, every additional hectare

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:22.460
of productive farmland depended on shipments

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.280
of Chilean salt peter or Peruvian guano.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.940
Those deposits were visibly shrinking

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.400
with each passing season.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.420
The atmosphere overhead held an inexhaustible reservoir

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:34.120
of nitrogen.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.000
Yet the triple bond that holds two nitrogen atoms together

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.340
resisted every ordinary chemical attack.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.260
Fritz Hovers 1909 demonstrations showed

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.340
that the bond could be broken under high pressure and temperature.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.400
Carl Bosch then scaled the same reaction

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.500
into continuous steel reactors.

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.900
Together, they removed the last absolute ceiling

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.360
on global food production.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.960
The shift replaced the supply chain whose costs were set

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.060
by geography and extraction rents.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.620
In its place came a system whose dominant expenses became

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:09.440
the energy to maintain the reaction conditions themselves.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.580
Farmers and agronomists had long treated nitrogen supply

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.700
as a mining and logistics problem.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.240
Guano islands of South America and the nitrate fields

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:22.820
of Chile's Atacama Desert were simply larger versions

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.000
of the manure piles and compost heaps

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.100
that had served agriculture for centuries.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.860
The convention assumed that usable nitrogen

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.880
had to be found already fixed in solid form.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.780
It then had to be transported across oceans

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.060
and spread on fields.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.820
That assumption locked the entire system

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.440
into a hard physical limit.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.940
The limit was set by how fast ships could move rock

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.780
and how large the remaining deposits proved to be.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.600
Prices fluctuated with every new discovery

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:52.540
and every labor strike at the ports.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.240
Yet the underlying premise that nitrogen must be dug up

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.540
remained unquestioned.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:01.840
By the early 1900s agronomists were openly calculating

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.000
how many additional billions of people the planet could feed

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.120
before the Chilean beds ran out.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.900
The industry therefore invested in bigger steam ships,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.580
deeper quarries and better port facilities.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.840
No one invested in an attempt to manufacture nitrogen compounds

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:18.640
from the air.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.800
The finished price of Chilean nitrate

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.040
delivered to European farms reflected those extraction

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.200
and shipping costs plus the rents earned

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.920
by the handful of companies that controlled the deposits.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.760
No one inside the trade asked what the atoms themselves

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.420
would cost if the triple bond could be forced open

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.420
by heat, pressure and a catalyst.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.920
The system treated the scarcity of fixed nitrogen

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.920
as an immutable fact of nature

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.340
rather than a solvable engineering problem.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.720
Transport alone added substantial cost

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.600
because the material had to move thousands of kilometers

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.540
and bulk before it reached the soil.

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.820
Seasonal shortages and price spikes were accepted

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.320
as normal business risks rather than signals

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.700
that the underlying chemistry needed rethinking.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.880
Haber began with the equilibrium constant

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.160
for the reaction of nitrogen and hydrogen to form ammonia.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:14.780
He asked what temperature and pressure would be required

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.100
to make the yield commercially interesting.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.740
The calculations showed that useful conversion demanded

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.000
pressures above 100 atmospheres

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.900
and temperatures above 400 degrees Celsius.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.100
Those conditions destroy ordinary steel

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:31.160
and most known catalysts.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.720
Bosch's team at BASF

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.880
therefore launched a systematic search

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.700
through thousands of metal oxides

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:39.600
and promoted iron formulations.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.480
They continued until they found a catalyst

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.800
that survived those extremes for months rather than hours.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.620
The magic wand floor for ammonia

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.200
is essentially the cost of the hydrogen atoms

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.640
plus the thermodynamic minimum energy needed

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.520
to break the nitrogen triple bond.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.620
On that basis, the raw material contribution

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:01.640
is only a few percent of the price

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.920
that ammonia commanded in 1913.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.720
The idiot index of the pre-haber supply chain

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.460
was therefore set by the cost of digging, shipping

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.560
and distributing to land nitrate.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:15.820
It was not set by any intrinsic scarcity

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.180
of nitrogen atoms.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.820
Once the high pressure route existed,

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.140
every subsequent engineering decision

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.860
could be judged by whether it reduced the energy

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:27.760
or the capital required to maintain those conditions.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:32.380
Bosch's first commercial reactor at Opao in 1913

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.820
replaced batch autoclaves with a continuous flow

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:36.840
of synthesis gas.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.080
The gas passed through a series of heat exchangers

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.860
that recovered the heat of reaction

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:43.520
to preheat incoming feed.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.480
Later units added better compressor designs

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:50.300
that cut the electricity needed to reach 200 atmospheres.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:53.860
Each incremental improvement in catalyst lifetime

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:56.760
or heat recovery directly lowered the energy term

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.740
that dominates the true floor.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.900
The process also required a steady supply of hydrogen.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.880
BASF initially obtained that hydrogen from coke oven gas

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:10.160
and later from steam reforming of natural gas.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.800
That step remains the largest single energy consumer

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.220
yet it is still far cheaper than importing

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.100
fixed nitrogen across oceans.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.760
Because the feedstock is now drawn from air

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.920
and methane rather than from a single geographic deposit,

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.940
the supply curve became essentially flat

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.600
once the reactors existed.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.780
A key trade-off was the need for extremely pure feed gases

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.480
to prevent catalyst poisoning.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.680
This required additional purification steps

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.060
that added capital but still proved cheaper than ocean freight.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.160
Another move was the progressive increase in reactor size

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.400
and operating pressure.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:49.180
That change improved throughput per vessel

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.100
and spread the fixed costs of the high pressure shell

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.660
over more tons of ammonia.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:56.460
Each of these changes attacked the energy

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.300
and capital components that set the actual floor

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.840
once the triple bond could be broken at all.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:04.340
The original Haber demonstration

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:06.660
used osmium and uranium catalyst

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.580
that were impractical at scale.

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.240
Bosch's team replaced them with iron based formulations

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.260
whose lower cost and longer life

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.540
moved the economics dramatically closer

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.100
to the thermodynamic minimum.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.520
The obvious objection that high pressure equipment itself

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.280
would be prohibitively expensive

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.400
was met by designing vessels whose wall thickness

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.100
and material selection were dictated

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:30.600
by the physics of the reaction

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.460
rather than by conventional fabrication limits.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.140
By the 1960s, the process had spread worldwide

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.060
and the delivered price of ammonia had fallen by more

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:43.260
than an order of magnitude relative to Chilean nitrate

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.400
in real terms.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:47.800
Today, roughly half the nitrogen atoms incorporated

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:51.300
into human tissue have passed through a Haber Bosch reactor.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.220
The idiot index has dropped dramatically

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:57.820
because the dominant cost is now the natural gas feedstock

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:01.680
and the compression energy rather than scarce mineral rents.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.840
Even so, the thermodynamic minimum energy

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:08.480
to split the triple bond still lies well below current practice.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:12.060
Modern plants achieve roughly 60% to 70%

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.680
of the theoretical efficiency

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.060
once heat recovery and catalyst performance

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:17.200
are taken into account.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:20.160
The remaining gap is set by the capital cost

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.280
of the high pressure vessels, the need for pure hydrogen

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.780
and the economic requirement that plants run continuously

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.100
at very large scale.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:31.260
Those constraints are real engineering limits,

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:32.820
not mere legacy habits.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:35.860
The process still depends on natural gas,

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.320
both as feedstock and as fuel,

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.040
so its environmental footprint tracks the carbon intensity

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.060
of that supply.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:47.420
Scaling further requires either cheaper renewable hydrogen

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.500
or continued gains in compression efficiency,

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.600
neither of which is trivial.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.220
The decisive move was to treat the nitrogen molecules

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.920
bond energy as the binding constraint

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:01.820
rather than the geography of existing deposits.

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.740
Once that constraint was attacked directly,

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.040
every downstream cost simply disappeared from the equation,

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.380
shipping, mining royalties and seasonal shortages

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:14.740
all vanished once the atoms could be rearranged at will.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:18.300
The same logic applies wherever an industry's price

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.460
is dominated by the cost of assembling

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.840
or transporting preformed components

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.940
instead of by the physics of the atoms themselves.

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:29.860
A second principle is that the first workable demonstration

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.000
must be followed by sustained engineering attention

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.980
to the energy and capital terms

00:08:34.980 --> 00:08:36.480
that actually set the floor.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:38.440
Without that follow through,

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.460
the initial breakthrough would have remained

00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:43.660
an expensive curiosity rather than a global industry.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:47.000
Habers equilibrium calculation identified

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.460
the thermodynamic target, Bosch's catalyst search

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:53.080
and reactor refinements then drove the process

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:56.680
toward that target, one incremental gain at a time.

00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:59.120
Each step lowered the idiot index

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:01.860
by replacing rents tied to scarce deposits

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.780
with costs tied to energy and capital

00:09:04.780 --> 00:09:06.440
that could be engineered downward.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:10.600
The result is visible in the order of magnitude price drop

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.900
and in the fact that half the nitrogen in human tissue

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:15.540
now flows through the process.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.860
Yet the remaining gap to the theoretical floor

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.860
shows that the work is never finished

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.820
once the first constraint is removed.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.080
New limits appear and must be attacked in turn.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.080
Who will next measure an entire supply chain

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.140
against the energy required to rearrange

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:32.700
its constituent molecules?

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.260
That's first principle's daily for today.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:37.960
If this changed how you see what things cost,

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:40.080
share it with someone who depreciate it

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:41.980
and subscribe wherever you listen.

00:09:42.500 --> 00:09:43.640
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:46.940
One example or one opportunity every day

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.680
see you tomorrow and if you'd rather watch

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.940
then listen find us on YouTube at Nerra Network.

